    "q-126868": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126868",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14131939/assets/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-6,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-8,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14131939/assets/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-4+(-6)+(-8) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
