      "source_example_id": "e-9557",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Circulation of a Vector Field Over a Region Containing Holes",
      "kp_id": "e-9557"
    },
    "q-126654": {
      "dom_id": "step-q126654",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14131939/assets/cbe865077b446bdd97c290cef0924d3f17807a9e1039c9be45f4aa6e4b29cc3c.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=5,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14131939/assets/fd221ae68fc4983fb0a3f7a745154764e5c0081b2b9b66e7f8ad4a150a8b3bc5.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the inner curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are opposite to those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =1-5-(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
