      "dom_id": "step-q126864",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14131939/assets/1253965689fbeb233ca3eda553cca8ea8bced9feb81ebc6565c9b65782066b47.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14131939/assets/cad0f92fdd888243562619b410a200ca040ca43f83bfd15fb124c8a8ecae4df6.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the inner curve ${C}_{2}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{2}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2-9+8 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
